## 1. Introduction
The increased adoption of the energy storage devices is driving the demand for lower cost, higher capacity and long lasting rechargeable batteries [1,2]. Lithium-ion batteries (LIBs) were successfully commercialized in mobile devices and electric vehicles (EVs) by virtue of their high specific capacity, cycling stability, as well as rate capability. For large-scale energy storage station (EES), however, sodium-ion batteries (SIBs) offer several advantages over LIBs: high abundance, low cost, while having similar electrochemical properties to Li [3–5].
In LIBs, the use of carbon [6], alloy [7], metal oxides [8,9], and metal sulfides [10] as anode materials and their lithium storage mechanisms were well-studied. However, problems such as huge volume expansion (e.g. Si [11–14], Sn [15,16]), low specific capacity (e.g. $\mathrm{Li}_{4}\mathrm{Ti}_{5}0_{12}$ [17], graphite [18,19]) and safety concerns for high voltage operation (e.g. metal oxides [9,20–22], and metal sulfides [23,24]) are issues to be considered before being able to be commercially applicable. Due to the similar electrochemical properties to Li, the above-mentioned problems also exist in SIBs [25].
Among these materials, the use of carbon materials, especially graphite, has received considerable attention due to its low cost, excellent chemical and thermal stability, and good conductivity. However, graphite has been unable to meet the future needs of energy storage due to its low energy density and low power density in LIBs. Although high capacities $(\sim300\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1},$ ) has been achieved using hard carbon as anode in SIBs, cycling stability at high rates is still poor. Thus, the research on anodes for SIBs were shifted towards the use of other carbon-based materials such as graphene [26–28], carbon nanotubes (CNT), porous carbon [29], heteroatomic doped carbon [30–32], biomass carbon [33–36] and metal-organic framework (MOF)-derived carbon [34,37].
Notably, MOF-derived carbon materials are of great interest due to its high specific area, evenly distributed pore size, ease of synthesis and structural tenability. For example, microcrystallinegraphene structure materials by a simple pyrolyzation of nitrogenous MOFs in a protective atmosphere allows high-content nitrogen doping [34]. Various MOF-derived carbons or derivatives included carbon nanoboxes [38], carbon nanospheres [39], carbon nanotubes [40] and others have been reported. It was reported that, a fish-scale carbon $\ @{\mathsf{C o}}_{3}0_{4}$ derived from Co-based MOF as a anode of LIBs displayed high capacity of ${\sim}612\ \mathrm{mAh}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $1.0\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ after 500 cycles [41]. In another report, a hollow carbon@ZnO obtained from the ZIF-8 exhibited outstanding cycling capability with the reversible capacity of $637.9\ \mathrm{mAh}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $1.0\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ after 500 cycles in LIBs [38]. Cube-shaped porous carbon derived from the MOF-5 delivered high reversible capacity $240\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $0.1\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ after 100 cycles as anode material of SIBs [37]. Structural adjustments and flexibilities in MOFs provide an unique avenue to develop a novel kind of highly tailorable derivatives. For example, $\mathsf{M W C N T s@C o_{3}O_{4}}$ [42], ${\sf G@C o_{3}O_{4}}$ [43], MWCNT@ZnO [44], $G/Z\mathrm{nO}/C$ [45], and $\mathrm{Mn}_{3}0_{4}@\mathrm{NPC}/\mathrm{G}$ [46] have been synthesized by using MOFs as templates, exhibiting high electrochemical performance in LIBs/SIBs. For instance, $\mathsf{M W C N T s@C o_{3}O_{4}}$ obtained from ZIF-67s/MWCNTs as template gives a high capacity of $813\mathrm{~mAh~g^{-1}}$ at $0.1\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ after 100 cycles. ${\bf G}/{\bf C}0_{3}0_{4}$ synthesized from ZIF-67s/GO precursor results in a higher rate behavior $(877\mathrm{~mAh}\mathrm{~g}^{-1}$ at $5\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}.$ ) and better stable cycling stability as compared to $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ . However, very little attention has given to the carbon/carbon composites materials derived from self-assembling MOFs with other materials. In spite of the progress achieved to date, MOFs-derived $N\mathrm{.}$ -doped porous carbon coated graphene has reported and exhibits an excellent performance as a anode for LIBs [47]. Therefore, more and better carbon/carbon composites should be designed and applied to durable energy storage devices.
Herein, we report the synthesis and structure-activity relationship of various $N\mathrm{.}$ -doped porous carbon nanocomposites (NPCNs) for LIB and SIB. These nanocomposites were synthesized by heat treatment of ZIF-8 grown on different carbon (graphene oxide, CNT and MCMB). As compared to other NPCNs, the NPC-CNT@G anode gives high electrochemical performances for both LIBs and
SIBs. As anode for LIBs, NPC-CNT $@G$ delivers high initial reversible capacity $(986\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $0.1\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ ) and excellent cycling stability $(542\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ after 2000 cycles at $5\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ ). It also exhibits high initial capacity of $315\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}}$ at $0.05\mathsf{A g}^{-1}$ and excellent cycling stability, with a capacity of $163\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}}$ after 300 cycles at $1\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ in SIBs. We believe that this work will provide further insights in developing better carbon/carbon composites materials for lithium and sodium storage.
## 2. Experimental
## 2.1. Materials preparation
## 2.1.1. Synthesis of NPC
All chemical reagents were AR (analytically grade). First, precursor ZIF-8 were obtained during the classical synthesis procedure, a $100\mathrm{ml}$ anhydrous methanol with $2.4\textrm{g}\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ was added dropwise to a $100\mathrm{ml}$ anhydrous methanol with $2.1\ \mathrm{g}$ 2-methylimidazole and $6{\mathrm{g}}$ polyvinylpyrrolidone (PVP) using a burette. Second, the mix solution was stirred for about $30\mathrm{min}$ and then stand for $20\mathrm{h}$ . Next, the product was washed many times with methanol and ID water and then drying in an oven at $80^{\circ}C$ to gain white powders (ZIF-8). Finally, $N\mathrm{.}$ -doped porous carbon obtained from ZIF-8 by a carbonizing process for $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ at $800^{\circ}\mathsf C$ in $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ atmosphere and the redundant zinc-ion are removed by diluted HCl solution.
## 2.1.2. Synthesis of NPC-CNT, NPC@G, and NPC-CNT@G
First, $_{0.1\mathrm{~g~}}$ functional carbon tube (F-CNT), graphene oxide (GO), and GO/F-CNT (2:1) were mixed in $100\mathrm{ml}$ methanol solution with $2.1\ \mathrm{g}$ 2-methylimidazole and $6{\mathrm{g}}$ PVP, respectively. The mixture was under an ultrasonic treatment for $0.5\mathrm{h}$ , and then uninterrupted stirring for $0.5\mathrm{h}$ to obtain stable suspensions. A $100\mathrm{ml}$ methanol solution with $2.4\mathrm{g}\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ was added dropwise to the above suspensions. Second, the mixed solution was stirred for about $30\mathrm{min}$ and then stand for $20\mathrm{h}$ . Second, the product was washed many times with methanol and DI water and then drying in an oven at $80^{\circ}C$ to gain gray powders (ZIF-8-CNT, ZIF$8@G$ , ZIF-8-CNT $@G$ ). Finally, $N\mathrm{\Omega}$ -doped porous carbon nanocomposites (NPCNs) obtained from gray powders by a carbonizing process for $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ at $800^{\circ}\mathsf C$ in $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ atmosphere and the redundant zinc-ion were removed by diluted HCl solution.
## 2.2. Materials characterizations
The X-ray sample diffraction of the prepared NCPNs electrodes were measured using a DX-2700BH X-ray diffractometer $\operatorname{Tu}\ \operatorname{K}\mathfrak{a}$ radiation: $\protect\uplambda=1.5406\mathring\mathrm{A}$ ). X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) analysis was performed on a PHI 5000 Versa probe system, using monochromatic Al $\mathsf{K}\mathsf{\pmb{\upalpha}}$ radiation $(1486.6\mathrm{eV})$ operating at $25\mathsf{W}.$ Raman measurements were performed using a Renishaw Raman spectrometer with a $532\mathrm{nm}$ laser. The morphology and microstructure (SEM) of the as-prepared NCPNs electrodes were characterized by field emission scanning electron microscopes (FEI). The HRTEM images were characterized by a transmission electron microscope (JEOL JEM-2100F). $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption–desorption measurements and were tested performed on a JW-BK132F Analyzer using the volumetric method.
## 2.3. Electrochemical measurement
Investigation of electrochemical measurement was taken by typical coin-half cells (R2032) with Li/Na metal foil as the counter electrode. The NPCNs electrodes were fabricated via stirring 80 wt $\%$ active materials, $10\mathrm{wt\%}$ super P, 10 wt% PVDF and NMP onto current collector (Cu foil) and then dried in vacuum at $90^{\circ}C$ overnight. 1 M $\mathrm{LiPF}_{6}$ in EC: DMC: FEC $(\mathsf{v}/\mathsf{v}=1\colon1\colon0.03)$ solution was used as the electrolyte of LIBs. And $1\mathrm{MNaClO}_{4}$ in EC: DEC $\begin{array}{r}{\left(\mathbf{V}/\mathbf{V}=1;1\right)}\end{array}$ solution was used as the electrolyte of SIBs. The R2032 cells were assembled in an Ar-filled glove box (Vigor 1G1200/750TS). Galvanostatic charge-discharge tests were performed on a LAND battery channel tester (CT2001A) under a voltage range of 0.01– $3\ensuremath{\mathrm{V}}$ (V vs. $\mathrm{Li^{+}/L i}$ ) and $0.01{-}2.5\:\mathrm{V}$ (V vs. $\Nu{\sf a}^{+}/\Nu{\sf a},$ under a current range of $0.1{-}10\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ (V vs. $\mathrm{Li^{+}/L i})$ and $0.1{-}2\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ (V vs. ${\mathsf{N a}}^{+}/{\mathsf{N a}},$ . Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) and cyclic voltammetry (CV) measurements were carried out on a PARSTAT 4000A or CHI660E electrochemical work-station.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. The structures and properties of NPCNs
The preparation of the NPCNs is depicted in Fig. 1a. It involves the electrostatic attraction between $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ and the electron-rich carbon substrates (CNT, GO, CNT/GO), followed by nucleation and growth of the ZIF-8 crystals. Carbonization and washing steps affords the NPCNs. The morphologies of the NPCNs were examined using FE-SEM and HRTEM (Fig. 1b–i). The structure of $N\mathrm{\Omega}$ -doped porous carbon (NPC) resembles that of the ZIF-8 particles (Fig. 1b and f). For NPC-CNT, the MWCNTs appear to be intertwined with the NPCs (Fig. 1c and g), The NPCs are found to be supported on the surface of the graphene $({\mathsf{N P C@G}})$ when GO is used as the substrate Fig. 1d and h). Interestingly, (Fig. 1e & i) show intertwined NPCs supported on the graphene surface when GO and CNT were used together. To demonstrate that this synthetic strategy can be utilized using other types of carbon, the NPC and NPC-CNT are assembled on the mesocarbon microbeats (MCMB) (NPC@MCMB and NPC-CNT in Fig. S1).
XRD spectra of the as-prepared nanocomposite-precursors (ZIF8-CNT, ZIF- $8@G$ and ZIF-8-CNT $@{\mathsf{G}}$ ) is corresponds to the XRD pattern of ZIF-8 (Fig. 2a). This implies that the ZIF-8 crystals were grown on carbon substrate. The XRD patterns of the NPCNs (NPC, NPC-CNT, $\mathsf{N P C@G}$ and NPC-CNT $@{\mathsf{G}}$ ) are shown in Fig. 2b. These carbon nanocomposites have diffraction peak at ${\sim}26^{\circ}$ , corresponding to that of graphitic carbon (0 0 2). The broadness or sharpness of the peak varies with the degree of graphitization [19]. The shape and signal intensity are a summation of the relative amounts of the different types of carbon in the material. Broad XRD peaks of NPC and $\mathsf{N P C@G}$ at $25.2^{\circ}$ and $26^{\circ}$ are observed. For NPC-CNT and NPC${\mathsf{C N T@G}}$ , the peaks consist of overlapping broad and sharp peaks. The sharp peak is corresponds to the CNT due to its higher degree of graphitization as compared to the NPC and G. The NPCNs are further characterized using Raman spectroscopy (Fig. 2b). The G band $(\sim1580\mathrm{cm}^{-1})$ is characteristic of $\mathsf{s p}^{2}$ bonded carbon. The D band $(\sim1339\mathrm{cm}^{-1})$ represents ring breathing mode of $\mathsf{s p}^{2}$ carbon ring, which is related to the level of defects and disorder in carbon. The $\mathrm{I_{D}/I_{G}}$ ratio for NPC-CNT $@6$ (0.91) is high than NPC-CNT (0.66) and lower than $\mathsf{N P C@G}$ (1.00). This is because (a) NPC and G has large amount of defects (b) CNT has a highly graphitized structure.
XPS analysis shows the doping state of N atoms in the carbon structure in Fig. 2d. Spectra deconvolution reveals the presence of, pyridinic N (N-6, $398.4\pm0.2\:\mathrm{eV}.$ ), pyrrolic N (N-5, $399.8\pm0.2\:\mathrm{eV}.$ and quaternary N $(400.7\pm0.4\mathrm{eV})$ [31,32,34]. The schematic for the different types of nitrogen doping states can be seen in Fig. 2d (right). N-5 links two $_{\mathsf{N-C}}$ bonds in a five membered ring. N-6 substitutes a carbon atom in the C6 ring and bonds with two $\mathsf{s p}^{2}$ carbon atoms. N-Q located inside the graphitic hexagonal lattice and bonds with three $\mathsf{s p}^{2}$ carbon atoms. The atomic percentages of N in NPC, NPC-CNT, $\mathsf{N P C@G}$ and NPC-CNT $@G$ are $22.3\%$ $16.1\%$ , $17.2\%$ , and $17.1\%$ , respectively. The hybrids have a lower N content as the NPCs are diluted by the carbon substrate (i.e CNT and G).
The specific surface areas and pore structures of NPCNs (NPC, NPC-CNT, $\mathsf{N P C@G}$ and NPC-CNT@G) are determined using $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ physisorption. The BET surface areas of NPC, NPC-CNT, $\mathsf{N P C@G}$ and NPC-CNT@G are 503.4, 571.6, 473.3 and $529.6\ensuremath{\mathrm{m}}^{2}\ensuremath{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ , with total pore volumes of 0.357, 0.462, 0.391, and $0.449\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively. In Fig. 2e, the curve of NPC displayed a typical type I isotherm, but the shape of NPC/C (CNT or G)-composites curves is typical type IV isotherm [48,49]. This phenomenon can be explained by pore-size distribution curve of NPCNs in Fig. 2f (obtained from $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ -adsorption isotherms via the DFT method). The NPCNs are found to have similar pore size distribution about $3.5\mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. 2f). The smaller pores $(\sim3.5\:\mathrm{nm})$ are likely to come from the ZIF-8-derived NPC particles, while the larger pores $(10-50\mathrm{nm})$ may be attributed to formation of the inter-particle mesopores. NPC-CNT $@6$ shows higher mesopores $10{-}50\mathrm{nm})$ adsorption than NPC which is well correspondent to type IV adsorption isotherm [48,49]. Taken together, this result reveals that the surface area and pore volume remains high even after the introduction of CNT and/or G into NPCNs.
3.2. The electrochemical performances of NPCNs electrodes in LIBs.
The CV curves and the specific capacity-voltage profiles for the NPCNs (NPC, NPC-CNT, $\mathsf{N P C@G}$ and NPC-CNT $@G$ ) were shown in Figs. 3a, b and S2. Fig. 3a displays the CV curves of NPC-CNT@G at a scan rate of $0.2\:\mathrm{mV}\:s^{-1}$ ranging from 0.1 to $3{\sf V}.$ . The reducing branches appear below $0.5\mathrm{V}$ that corresponds to Li-insertion of NPC-CNT@G. The broad oxidizing branches summits are observed at about $0.5\mathrm{V}$ that corresponds to the Li-extraction of NPC${\mathsf{C N T@G}}$ . The reducing peak located at about $0.3\mathrm{-}0.7\:\mathrm{V}$ at the 1st cycle is different from the subsequent cycles due to the formation of solid electrolyte interphase (SEI) membrane on electrode. This phenomenon is also observed in other NPCNs (shown in Fig. S3).
The decomposition voltages of the electrolyte for NPCNs are triggered by the different surfaces and pore structures of NPCNs.
The electrochemical performances of NPCNs electrode are evaluated using CR2032-type coin cells in the voltage range of $0.01{-}3\mathrm{V}$ at a low current rate of $0.1\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ . The steady current charge/discharge curve of NPC-CNT $@6$ electrode is shown in Fig. 3b. The initial discharge capacity is $1756\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ with a high reversible capacity of $986\mathrm{mAh}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , corresponding to an initial coulombic efficiency (ICE) of $56.2\%$ . For comparisons, the steady current charge/discharge curves of NPC, NPC-CNT and $\mathsf{N P C@G}$ in Fig. S3 deliver reversible capacities of 978, 891 and $871\ \mathrm{mAh}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively. Furthermore, the initial charge capacities of NPC, NPC-CNT and $\mathsf{N P C@G}$ are $53.4\%$ , $56.8\%$ and $62.3\%$ . The consumptions of lithium ions and formation of SEI layer lead to the irreversible capacity appear in the 1st cycle. Fig. 3c displays short cycling performances of NPCNs electrodes. Different structures have different cycling properties, obviously. For the NPC, it only shows a low reversible capacity of $871\ \mathrm{mAh}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . Then it quickly drops to $753\mathrm{~mAh~g^{-1}}$ after 20 cycles, then gradually decreases to $627\mathrm{~mAh~g^{-1}}$ after 100 cycles. For the NPC-CNT electrode, an approximate reversible discharge capacity of $891\ \mathrm{mAh}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ is obtained, and presents a more stable cycling performance that remaining a capacity of $784\mathrm{~mAh~g^{-1}}$ after 100 cycles. In addition, the NPC@G demonstrated an excellent reversible capacity is $978\mathrm{~mAh~g^{-1}}$ and mildly growing cycling at $0.1\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ . The capacity gradually increase in the following cycles, reaching a capacity of $1026.4\mathrm{mAh}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ after 100 cycles. The mildly growing capacity may be caused by the electrochemical activation. The NPC${\mathsf{C N T@G}}$ reveals a reversible capacity of $986\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , and still reaching a capacity of $924\mathrm{~mAh~g^{-1}}$ after 100 cycles ( $94\%$ capacity retention). These results indicate that NPC-CNT $@G$ possesses the stable cycling performance of NPC-CNT, together with the high reversible capacity of NPC-G.
The rate performance of the NPCN electrodes is rigorously examined with gradual increase at the current rate from 0.1 to $10\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ (Fig. 3d). Among the NPCNs, NPC-CNT $@6$ gives the best rate capability with a high reversible capacities of 949, 898, 751, 637, 529, 443 and $336\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at 0.1, 0.2, 0.5, 1, 2, 5 and $10\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ , respectively (as shown in Figs. 3e and S3). Additionally, it is still reaching a capacity of $922\mathrm{mAh}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $0.1\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ after three cycle rate capability test (0.1 to $10\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ ). Fig. 3f shows the long term cycling performance of NPCNs, tested at $0.1\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ for the first 5 cycles and at $5\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ for the next 1995 cycles. NPC-CNT $@G$ anode delivers stability of cycling at high current of $5\mathsf{A g}^{-1}$ . Compare with other NPCNs (include NPC, NPC-CNT, $\mathsf{N P C@G}$ ), the NPC${\mathsf{C N T@G}}$ gives the highest initial capacity of $489\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $5\mathsf{A g}^{-1}$ , steadily increasing to $603\mathrm{~mAh~g^{-1}}$ and stabilizing to $542\ \mathrm{mAh}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ after 2000 cycles. This phenomenon often boils down to the electrochemical activation. All plots for the EIS of NPCNs in Fig. $3\mathrm{g}$ show the combination of a half-baked semicircle at the high frequency region (electron-transfer resistance) and an oblique line at the low frequency region ( $\mathrm{\DeltaLi^{+}}$ diffusion resistance). Compared with the other NPCNs, the NPC-CNT $@G$ anode owns the much smaller charge-transfer resistances $(160.8\Omega)$ .
The experimental results on NPCNs electrode indicate that the comprehensive lithium-storage performance of CNT/grapheme/ NPC structure is better than other (NPC-CNT, $\mathsf{N P C@G}$ and NPC). Opposite with the NPC, the excellent capacity performance of $\mathsf{N P C@G}$ can be ascribed to the NPC nanoparticles orderly arranged in the surface of graphene. The orderly arranged NPC nanoparticles make it possible for release of capacity effectively. And the excellent improvements of NPC-CNT in the rate performance are caused by the establishment of a conductive path which is by CNTs in situ inserted into the NPC nanoparticles. The NPC-CNT $@G$ turns out to be more efficient than the previously proposed NPC-CNT and $\mathsf{N P C@G}$ . Moreover, the structure multi-dimensional of NPC${\mathsf{C N T@G}}$ combines the advantages of NPC nanoparticles, graphene and CNTs. The synergistic effect of the three causes the electrochemical performance to intensify further.
## 3.3. The electrochemical performances of NPCNs electrodes in SIBs
The tailored NPCNs, as discussed above for lithium storage, are also supposed to have excellent capacity retention in sodium storage. Therefore, the electrochemical performance of the NPCNs electrodes are further investigated by Na-coin cells. The CV curves of NPCNs are reflected in Figs. 4a and S4. For the first cycle, a broad reducing peak about $0.1\mathrm{-}0.6\:\mathrm{V}$ is different from those peaks of subsequent cycles due to the formation of SEI membrane on the electrode. In subsequent cycles, the main reducing peak becomes below $\ensuremath{0.7\mathrm{V}}$ that corresponds well to the sodiation process of NPC-CNT $@{\mathsf{G}}$ . As exhibited in the Fig. 4b, the capacity-voltage profiles of the NPC-CNT $@{\mathsf{G}}$ in SIBs are tested at $0.05\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ . The first discharge/charge capacity of NPC-CNT $@G$ is $315/727\mathrm{mAh}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , corresponding to the ICE of $43\%$ . The initial irreversible capacity drop, as discussed above in the CV section, can be associated with consume a number of Na-ions to form the SEI layer.
The rate performance of the NPCNs electrode is performed at different current rates of $0.05{-2}\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ (Figs. 4e, S5 and S6). Among all the NPCNs electrode, the rate performance of the NPC-CNT $@6$ electrode in SIBs is superior than NPC@G, NPC-CNT and NPC and it delivers with a high reversible capacities of 315, 284, 249, 208, 174, and $146\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}}$ at 0.1, 0.2, 0.5, 1 and $2\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ , respectively. The cycle performance of the NPCNs electrodes is tested by short cycling performance at small current of $0.1\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ (Fig. 4c) and long cycling performance at high current of $1\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ (Fig. 4f). In the Fig. 4c and d, the NPC-CNT $@{\mathsf{G}}$ , just like in LIBs, also demonstrates that a best cycling performance in SIBs with a high charge capacity of $257\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}}$ after 100 cycles at $0.1\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ ( $87\%$ capacity retention) and $163\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}}$ after 300 cycle at $1\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ $80\%$ capacity retention). In Fig. 4d, the NPC-CNT $@{\mathsf{G}}$ electrode reveals the optimal electrical conductivity and the minimum resistances of electrontransfer $(25.8\Omega)$ among all NPCNs electrode in SIBs. These results, similar to EIS in LIBs, are demonstrated the three dimensional conductive frame by grapheme and CNT also applies to SIBs.
Table 1 compares the performance of NPC-CNT $@6$ with other carbon-based materials, included rGO, CNT, biomass-carbon, $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}$ composite, and $\cos_{2}/C$ composites, etc., as the anode for LIBs and SIBs in previously reports. The overall property of NPC${\mathsf{C N T@G}}$ takes a favorable position both LIBs and SIBs among listed materials. Furthermore, the synthesis of 3D hierarchical-structure carbon/carbon composites can be controlled by various carbon substrates. It is concluded that more carbon/carbon hierarchicalstructure should be developed for high performance LIBs or SIBs through MOFs self-assembling strategy.
3.4. Electrochemical kinetics analysis of the NPC-CNT@G for SIBs and structural evolution mechanism in LIBs and SIBs
High surface area dominated nanocarbons are often applied for sodium storage, which is favorable of ${\mathsf{N a}}^{+}$ insertion or adsorption in a certain range of dissimilarly chemical or physical storage sites [35,62]. As mentioned above, the Fig. 5a indicated the structural merits about the charge transportation and sodium storage mechanism are graphically. The high surface area $(529\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ ), and abundantly $N\mathrm{.}$ -doped defect $17\%$ N content) of the NPC-CNT $@6$ contributes to its high sodium-storage capacity, which can supply Na storage in a range of active sites [32,62,63]. And broad mesopore distribution $(10-50\mathrm{nm})$ and implant of conductive channel (CNTs) are beneficial to improve the rate of sodium ion transfer to active sites in the NPC nanoparticles by providing stable and fast tracks for sodium ion from the electrolyte [64–66]. As a result, the three-dimensional conductive frame from by nanoparticles, graphene and CNTs enabled the NPC-CNT $@6$ more capabilities to avail the $\mathsf{N a}^{+}$ into capacitance, which further improves the rate capability and cycling stability [67]. In order to verify this point, electrochemical kinetics behavior is demonstrated in Fig. 5b to e. In Fig. 5b, CV curves of different scan rates between 0.1 and $2\mathsf{m}\mathsf{V}\mathsf{s}^{-1}$ are used to investigate the pseudocapacitive-like property of NPCCNT@G. The peak current (ip) and scan rate (v) match with a power-law formula [68]:
$$
\dot{\mathbf{i}}=\mathbf{av}^{\mathrm{b}}.
$$
The b-value and a-value are adjusted to the slope and intercept of the log (|i|) – log (v) oblique line via the formula. Empirically, the b-value $=0.5$ and 1 each represent diffusion-controlled process and surface capacitive-controlled process. After data fitting, the bvalues of cathodic and anodic peaks are 0.63 and 0.89 (Fig. 5c), respectively. As a result, capacitive reactions co-exist with insertion in sodium-ion storage of NPC-CNT@G. The surface capacitive-controlled process obviously holds a dominant position for charge storage, just as the similar result in previous reports [69,70], suggesting the adsorption of Na-ion on the surface of NPC-CNT $@6$ . The 3D structure and defective sites in NPC-CNT@G becomes the main factor for sodium-ion storage of NPC-CNT@G. To calculate the contributions of capacitive-controlled capacities and diffusion-controlled capacities, the following equation:
$$
\mathbf{i}(\mathbf{V})=\mathbf{k}_{1}\nu+\mathbf{k}_{2}\nu^{1/2}
$$
is often applied to separate the two kinds of storage mechanisms. The $\mathbf{k}_{1}\nu$ -value and $\mathrm{k}_{2}\nu^{1/2}$ -value are separated into capacitive-controlled effects and insertion at fixed-voltage $\mathrm{i}(\mathsf{V})$ Fig. 5d differentiates CV curve of NPC-CNT ${\widehat{\ @}}G$ at $1\mathrm{mV}s^{-1}$ into a capacitive-controlled region (blue) and a diffusion-controlled region (red). The contribution ratios of capacitive process at various scan rates are shown in Fig. 5e. The contributions of capacitive-controlled region are increased gradually with scan rates, which is from $55\%$ at $0.5\mathrm{mV}s^{-1}$ to $75\%$ at $2\mathrm{m}\mathrm{v}s^{-1}$ . The capacitive-controlled process on the surface and rich active defect sites of NPC-CNT $@G$ is conducive to fast Na-ions store. Therefore, the high capacity of NPC-CNT@G is attributed to the synergistic effect of adsorption and insertion and the excellent rate performance is results of Na adsorption.
In addition, the morphological of tailored NPC-CNT@G have clearly structural transformations after manifold cycles in SIBs and LIBs. As vividly depicted in Fig. 6a, the NPC nano-particles in NPC-CNT $@6$ electrode converted to connective the sponge-like NPC cluster. This converted process graphically illustrated by the SEM photograph of NPC-CNT $@{\mathsf{G}}$ electrode after different cycles in
Fig. 6b. In the meantime, the electrochemical EIS tests are used for track the converted process. As described in Fig. 6d to f, the entirety resistances $\left(\mathsf{R}_{\mathrm{ct}}+\mathsf{R}_{\mathrm{SEI}}\right)$ of NPC-CNT@G electrode are decrease with cycles after 10 cycles. The resistances changes are correspondence with structural transformations. The sponge-like NPC cluster structure after the cycles has batter conductivity and more stable structure. This structural evolution during cycling is beneficial and a key factor for the tailored NPCNs that have strong cycling stabilities.
## 4. Conclusions
Through a simple and efficient approach based on the selfassembly of ZIF-8 on graphene-based carbon, a series of $N\mathrm{.}$ -doped porous carbon nanocomposites (NPC, NPC-CNT, $\mathsf{N P C@G}$ and NPC${\mathsf{C N T@G}}$ ) were synthesized. Compared with bare NPC, the electrochemical performance of carbon substrates using (CNT, G) together with NPC was enhanced, which delivers $986\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at the current of $0.1\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ in LIBs ( $315\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at the current of $0.05\mathsf{A g}^{-1}$ in SIBs) and the $99\%$ capacity retention after the 2000 cycles at $5\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ in LIBs $80\%$ capacity retention after the 300 cycles at $1\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ ). Furthermore, these performances indicate that electrical conductivity may be contribution to specific capacities. Amongst the NPCNs, NPC-CNT $@G$ is the best electrode for LIBs and SIBs. The electrochemical performance of NPC-CNT $@G$ is also better or comparable with other LIB and SIB electrodes in the literature. We believe that the synthetic strategy used in this work can be utilized for preparing other novel structures for high Li and Na storage.
